WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:06.400
]Marvel Studios' “Eternals” is a big movie, with&nbsp;
big heroes, an epic storyline and cinematography&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:12.160
screaming to be seen in IMAX. As such, it’s easy&nbsp;
to lose track of the film’s smaller characters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:15.360
Here are some "Eternals" characters&nbsp;
who mean more than you realize.

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:15.840
"Let's go!"

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:21.200
Remember when Scarlett Johansson appeared in&nbsp;
“Iron Man 2”? It was noteworthy, for sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:25.360
because Johansson was already a big star&nbsp;
with films like “Lost in Translation”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:30.000
and would-be blockbusters like “The Island”&nbsp;
under her belt. But her first appearance as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.640
Natasha Romanoff felt like the character was&nbsp;
intended to be a scene-stealing sidekick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:39.680
not someone who’d go on to become a top MCU&nbsp;
character and get her own spin-off movie.

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:44.480
There’s reason to believe that Kit Harington’s&nbsp;
Dane Whitman could be set for a similar ascension&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:49.840
in this newest MCU phase. In “Eternals,”&nbsp;
it’s almost laughable how non-heroic he is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:54.080
existing for two apparent reasons: to&nbsp;
ask the questions fans want answered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:55.680
and to be a supportive boyfriend.

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.800
“Why didn’t you guys help fight Thanos? Or any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:00.880
war, or all the other terrible&nbsp;
things throughout history?”

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:06.800
As Sersi reveals to Dane that she is a god-like&nbsp;
being, has been on Earth for 7000 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:10.240
and has an ex-boyfriend who could&nbsp;
disintegrate him with the blink of an eye,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:13.600
Dane just makes goo goo eyes and&nbsp;
takes her for long, romantic walks.

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:15.360
"How long were you together?"

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:16.640
"5000 years."

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:17.760
"I guess you can call that 'long-term.'"

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:22.560
Which feels a little beneath the “Game of&nbsp;
Thrones” actor who just finished playing Jon Snow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:26.800
Lord Commander of the Night’s Watch, King&nbsp;
in the North, Lord of the Seven Kingdoms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:31.440
and Protector of the Realm. Now Kit&nbsp;
Harington is just ... a boyfriend?

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:35.200
Perhaps this apparent insignificance&nbsp;
is because, in scenes we never witness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:40.000
he is indeed on his own hero’s journey throughout&nbsp;
the film. Near the end of the film, Dane tells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.640
Sersi his family history is "complicated”&nbsp;
and, as any Marvel fan with a smartphone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:49.600
and a search engine can tell you, “Dane Whitman”&nbsp;
will one day become a hero called "Black Knight."

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:52.960
Of course, the most obvious reveal that&nbsp;
Kit Harington will soon be wielding the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:57.680
legendary Ebony Blade sword and riding on&nbsp;
horseback is the second post-credits scene.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:02.080
We see Dane addressing a family legacy that&nbsp;
stretches back to the days of King Arthur.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:06.960
Examining the Ebony Blade, he delivers&nbsp;
the ominous phrase “Death is my reward,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:10.880
before an off-camera voice interrupts him&nbsp;
with “Are you sure you’re ready for that?”

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:16.000
Rumor has it that Dane’s story will be continued&nbsp;
in 2022’s “Doctor Strange in the Multiverse of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:20.480
Madness.” In the comics, the Black Knight not&nbsp;
only becomes an Avenger, but is known for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:25.920
ability to navigate different time periods and&nbsp;
alternate realities. Taking all this into account,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:30.560
you can see how Harington’s character could become&nbsp;
instrumental over the next several Marvel films.

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:36.960
On the surface, Kingo's valet-slash-filmographer&nbsp;
feels like a walking punchline. Every time Kingo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:41.120
does something heroic, Karun is there to&nbsp;
assure the Eternal that he got the shot;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:45.520
every time someone smashes his camera, Karun&nbsp;
hilariously seems to whip out another one.

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:47.280
"Kingo the movie star!"

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:49.280
"I've directed some things too."

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:51.520
But look closer, and you’ll see the one human that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:54.640
the Eternals have come to trust&nbsp;
more than they ever have before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:59.280
over thousands of years. Granted, it’s not like&nbsp;
they have much choice — Kingo insists that his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:04.160
valet come along for the reunion — but over time,&nbsp;
they begin warming to the man behind the camera.

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:08.400
When the smoke clears, Karun possesses not&nbsp;
only the friendship and trust of the Eternals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:11.600
but an incredibly valuable&nbsp;
assemblage of raw footage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:15.680
Imagine being in possession of film footage&nbsp;
that would immediately render all theology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:20.320
religion and world history irrelevant.&nbsp;
What will Karun do with this footage?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:24.560
What is he willing to do with it? And would&nbsp;
he do anything without Kingo’s approval?

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:28.240
By the end of the film, the world likely&nbsp;
knows that some major events have occurred,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:32.720
but it’s debatable whether the Eternals have&nbsp;
“revealed” themselves. It seems that as their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:37.920
story moves along in the MCU, their existence&nbsp;
will become common knowledge; don’t be surprised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:42.560
if all that seemingly-comic-relief footage shot&nbsp;
by Karun is what drags them into the spotlight.

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:47.440
While “Eternals” breaks multiple boundaries&nbsp;
within the Marvel Cinematic Universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:51.280
it treats most of these exactly the&nbsp;
way they should be treated: as normal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:56.160
everyday life. One of these is a relationship&nbsp;
between Phastos and his husband Ben, who the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:00.640
other Eternals learn have been building a life&nbsp;
together in Chicago with their adorable son Jack.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:04.480
"Dad! That's Superman! With the cape! And you&nbsp;
were shooting laser beams out of your eyes!"&nbsp;

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:06.560
"I don't wear a cape."

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:12.480
This is the first time a same sex couple&nbsp;
has been featured in a Marvel Studios film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:17.040
Their kiss, as Phastos is headed off to save&nbsp;
the world, is not only a powerful moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:22.480
but a significant one for the LGBTQ community.&nbsp;
Considering how many millions of people all over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:27.600
the globe watch every Marvel film, it also means&nbsp;
a great deal personally to actor Haaz Sleiman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:32.480
who came out as gay in 2017. The actor&nbsp;
explained to Variety at the film’s premiere,

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:37.120
“Beyond a dream come true, it’s life-saving. I&nbsp;
wish I had that [representation] when I was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:42.640
kid [...] My god. I wish! Can you imagine how&nbsp;
many lives this is going to be saving — kids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:47.360
young queer folk who are being bullied [...] And&nbsp;
now they get to see this — it’s above and beyond.”

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:49.680
According to writer/director Chloe Zhao,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:54.000
Marvel had already laid out a blueprint for&nbsp;
the family of Phastos when she came on board.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:58.160
Finding true love after thousands of years&nbsp;
returns a faith in humanity to Phastos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:03.280
and leads to his personal decision to not only&nbsp;
fight for humans, but eventually return to them.

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:08.320
So on one level, Ben might feel just like&nbsp;
another “supportive spouse” character in the MCU,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:11.200
no different than Hawkeye’s&nbsp;
wife Laura. On another level,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:14.960
that might make him one of the most important&nbsp;
characters we’ve ever seen in the MCU.

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:21.280
Sprite is among the very first Eternals,&nbsp;
debuting in 1976’s “Eternals” issue 9.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:25.680
But in the comics, she is typically a he,&nbsp;
who inspired both Shakespeare (who created&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:30.080
the character Puck in his image) and J.M.&nbsp;
Barrie (who did the same with Peter Pan).

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:34.320
Because “Eternals” features 10 main heroes,&nbsp;
there isn’t a lot of time for deep dives into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:38.960
these characters. As such, Sprite feels like&nbsp;
one of the Eternals that gets short shrift.

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:43.840
In the comics, Sprite is much more fully-realized,&nbsp;
and employs illusions much more frequently.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:47.920
Every bit as mischievous as Loki, Sprite&nbsp;
also possesses matter-manipulating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:51.760
abilities similar to Sersi, and is&nbsp;
strong enough to lift several tons.

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:56.160
In “Eternals,” Sprite is compared to the&nbsp;
Peter Pan story — not as its inspiration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.160 --> 00:06:00.000
but because the way she secretly yearns&nbsp;
to be with Ikaris is reminiscent of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:02.640
Tinkerbell can never be with&nbsp;
the boy who wouldn’t grow up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:07.520
Ultimately, however, the MCU Sprite feels&nbsp;
more like Pinocchio, who yearned to be real.

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:12.320
In “Eternals Volume 5” issue 1, released just&nbsp;
a few months before the movie was released,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:16.000
it was revealed that the Eternals&nbsp;
switch gender every few thousand years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:20.320
This is never mentioned in the movie, but the&nbsp;
fan favorite character is depicted as being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:25.160
female when she first appears on Earth, as well&nbsp;
as thousands of years later in the modern age.

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:30.640
Another Eternal who gets the Reader’s&nbsp;
Digest-treatment in the feature film is Gilgamesh.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:35.280
Another comic character whose history with&nbsp;
the title stretches back to the mid-‘70s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:38.320
that history has intermingled&nbsp;
with Hercules, Achilles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:43.120
Buffalo Bill and King David — and for a&nbsp;
time, he even fought for the Roman Empire.

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:47.360
Perhaps it is appropriate, in a way, that he&nbsp;
seems to receive the least screen time of any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:51.280
of the Eternals. In the pages of Marvel&nbsp;
Comics, he was punished for many years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:54.960
after learning about the true nature&nbsp;
of his heritage. Confined to Olympia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:59.520
he was branded “The Forgotten One,” and&nbsp;
was subsequently ostracized for centuries.

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:03.360
In the movie, Gilgamesh is a chef that&nbsp;
enjoys preparing bountiful feasts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:07.760
and has also taken on the personal&nbsp;
task of becoming a caretaker for Thena.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:12.480
He also wields a big powerful punch, which&nbsp;
feels a bit redundant after Marvel audiences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:16.240
have already seen several other characters&nbsp;
who can do essentially the same thing.

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:19.680
In the film, each Eternal is depicted&nbsp;
as having their own unique power;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:24.640
in the comics, Gilgamesh, who joined the Avengers&nbsp;
for a brief period, also has superhuman speed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:29.760
can shoot beams of cosmic energy, and can use his&nbsp;
telekinetic power to take flight. Which is all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:34.720
very impressive, but perhaps not nearly as much as&nbsp;
that meal he makes for the Eternals in Australia.

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:36.800
"This is really good, Gil."

